        "heading": "IDS vs IPS: Where Each Sits in the Path",
        "content": "The functional difference between these two systems comes down entirely to where the box physically sits relative to the traffic. An IPS sits inline, directly in the path every packet must cross, which is what gives it the power to block a connection outright — and the same reason a false-positive signature is so much costlier there than anywhere else. An IDS sits off to the side, watching a mirrored copy of the same traffic, which means it can alert on exactly the same signature but never touch the packet itself. Neither position is universally correct; the choice is a deliberate tradeoff between detection speed and blast radius.",
